-  <p><code>--monitor 3</code> is the setting to pass, and the default. After your lead's approved push, it pushes its own review fixes without asking again. If your lead wants to see those fixes first, use <code>--monitor 2</code>. Without the Augment Code review app on the repository, or if you are not sure it is installed (the repository's Settings &gt; GitHub Apps page lists it), use <code>--monitor 0</code>, which only opens the PR.</p>
+  <p><strong>Choose exactly one monitor ceiling</strong> for the job and pass it explicitly (guide 3.11): <code>--monitor 0</code> only opens the PR (use it when the review app is missing or you are not sure it is installed); <code>--monitor 1</code> proposes fixes without editing source; <code>--monitor 2</code> makes the fixes locally and stops before pushing; <code>--monitor 3</code>, the command's current default, runs the full loop you authorized: it fixes confirmed review findings of Medium severity or higher (lower ones are only reported) and failing CI checks (the automatic tests and checks GitHub runs on the PR), pushes those fixes, and replies to and resolves the threads it fixed.</p>
+  <p>The push rule (guide section 4): after your lead's approved push, <code>--monitor 3</code> pushes its own review fixes without asking again. If your lead wants to see those fixes first, use <code>--monitor 2</code>. A stronger project rule can require monitor 3; if that conflicts with a lower ceiling you were asked for, the guide says to disclose the conflict and not run it.</p>
